// ============================================================================================
// DISPATCH MODELS (`find: "dispatch"`). A model here attaches to a CALL SITE's static
// signature, not to a callee, and it REPLACES the site's target set: one summary row instead of
// every implementation CHA can reach. That is what makes it worth having -- `toString` alone is
// about a sixth of a median app's excess call-graph edges -- and it is also its whole risk,
// because the callee bodies leave the analysis along with the edges.
//
// So the list is short and every entry is a CONTRACT: a method whose behaviour is fixed by the
// interface it implements, not by which implementation runs. Anything whose implementations are
// unrelated fragments of the program -- `FunctionN.invoke`, `Runnable.run`, `Provider.get`,
// generated serializers -- is deliberately absent. Those exceed the CHA threshold and fall to
// hybrid inlining by themselves, which is what recovers the receiver's identity.
//
// A model is refused for a signature whose targets hold a matched source or sink, and the site
// takes CHA instead; `ctadl index` says so. Pass the same --models to `index` and `query`.
//
// THE PORTS mean what they mean on a `find: methods` propagation: the receiver is Argument(0),
// the nth argument is Argument(n), the result is Return. Most entries below are the `find:
// methods` entry above with `find` changed.
//
// The `Object` models are faithful, not merely cheap. Over 204 named-field writes across all
// four contract methods in two apps, 181 went into an object reached from the receiver's own
// field, 13 into an object allocated in the body, 10 unattributed, and none into an argument or
// a static. See /Volumes/Shampoo/ctadl-sweep/sigstudy/purity.py.
// ---- the Object contract ----
{"find":"dispatch","where":[{"constraint":"signature_match","name":"toString"},{"constraint":"signature","pattern":"^\\(\\)Ljava/lang/String;$"}],"model":{"propagation":[{"input":"Argument(*)","output":"Return"}]}}
// hashCode and equals are models, not skips: the primitive is derived from the receiver's
// CONTENTS, and a configuration that follows an integer is entitled to.
{"find":"dispatch","where":[{"constraint":"signature_match","name":"hashCode"},{"constraint":"signature","pattern":"^\\(\\)I$"}],"model":{"propagation":[{"input":"Argument(*)","output":"Return"}]}}
{"find":"dispatch","where":[{"constraint":"signature_match","name":"equals"},{"constraint":"signature","pattern":"^\\(Ljava/lang/Object;\\)Z$"}],"model":{"propagation":[{"input":"Argument(*)","output":"Return"}]}}
{"find":"dispatch","where":[{"constraint":"signature_match","name":"clone"},{"constraint":"signature","pattern":"^\\(\\)Ljava/lang/Object;$"}],"model":{"propagation":[{"input":"Argument(0)","output":"Return"}]}}
