// ---- resource release: hybrid inlining, not a model ----
// A `close()` body is where buffered data is finally handed to a file or a socket, which is
// where a sink model lives -- and the source/sink guard cannot see a sink reached THROUGH a
// target. A model of any shape here would keep taint out of that body. Hybrid inlining keeps
// the body reachable and resolves the receiver precisely when its allocation is visible, which
// for a stream it usually is. Not plain CHA either: about a fifth of an app's `close` overrides
// write a named field, and with up to 176 targets these sites would exceed the threshold and
// defer anyway; forcing it makes the disposition the same at every site of the signature. A
// site with exactly one target still takes CHA, so this never makes an exact site worse.
